He also organised his saṁnyāsin disciples and the tradition of अस्चेतिच्स् through them, into ten orders, known as ‘daśanāmī-सम्प्रदाय’, (daśa = ten, nāmī = with name; hence ‘daśanāmī’) of the following titles:
Araṇya;
Āśrama;
Bhāratī;
Giri;
Parvata;
Purī;
Sarasvatī;
Sāgara;
Tīrtha and
Vana.
Every saṁnyāsin of these orders adds to his adopted name one of these ten titles as a suffix. For instance: विद्यारण्य, Nṛsiṁhabhārati, आनन्दगिरि, तोतापुरी, Ānandatīrtha and so on.
These ten orders have been specifically aligned with the four maṭhas as follows:
Jyotirmaṭha—Giri, Parvata and Sāgara.
Śāradāmaṭha—Āśrama and Tīrtha.
Govardhanamaṭha—Araṇya and Vana.
Śṛṅgerīmaṭha—Bhāratī, पुरी and सरस्वती.
Though it is difficult to derive any definite conclusions as to the meanings of these ten terms which may not be related to their normal meanings, traditional interpretations and explanations are on the following lines:
Araṇya indicates a monk living in an araṇya or a forest, completely detached from mundane affairs.
Āśrama indicates a monk living in an आश्रम or a hermitage thus freed from the botheration of wandering.
Bhāratī indicates one laden with learning, who has thrown away all burdens.
Giri indicates one who is steady like a mountain (= giri) or one who lives at the foot of a mountain.
Parvata indicates one who lives at the foot of hills and has steadfast knowledge regarding the transient nature of the world.
Purī indicates one who is full of the knowledge of ब्रह्मन्. (Purī अस्चेतिच्स् seem to have been considered as the highest among the मोन्क्स्.)
Sarasvatī indicates one who is greatly learned and also an adept in योग.
Sāgara indicates one who has dived into the depths of the ocean of knowledge and collected the gems of truth.
Tīrtha indicates one who bathes in the confluence of the three rivers of the knowledge and the realisation of the truth ‘tat tvam asi’ (‘That thou art’).
Vana indicates one who has transcended all the snares of desires and lives quietly in a forest.

The Daśanāmī मोन्क्स् are further classified into four sampradāyas or traditional sects or groups without reference to their orders, but as assigned to the four monastic centres. They are:
Ānandavāra attached to the Jyotirmaṭha;
Bhogavāra attached to the Govardhanamaṭha;
Bhūrivāra attached to the Śṛṅgerīmaṭha;
Kīṭavāra attached to the Śāradāmaṭha.
These terms (though they appear to be a little fanciful) respectively indicate freedom from
desire for sense-pleasures;
desire for all enjoyments;
desire for worldly possessions;
tendencies hurtful to creatures.
Each of these four monastic centres, again, has its own devatā and देवी (tutelary deities), क्षेत्र (place of पिल्ग्रिमगे), tīrtha (holy river), गोत्र (spiritual lineage), Veda, स्वाध्याय (study of parti-cular उपनिषद्स्) and a mahāvākya (sentence for contemplation chosen from the उपनिषद्स्). The table given in page no. 467 gives all the details in a nutshell.
In establishing the Daśanāmī order of मोन्क्स्, as attached to the four maṭhas, शङ्कर showed remarkable social awareness also which was badly needed during his times. These maṭhas were charged with the task of caring for the welfare of the people of the regions assigned to them, through tours and discourses.
